MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m20",
  "summary": "Describe seat belts, airbags, body components, trim and accessories, handle restraint systems safely, and recognise collision damage and the causes of noises and water leaks.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe seat belts, pretensioners, load limiters and child restraints, and inspect a belt.","Explain how an airbag system works and follow the precautions for working near it.","Describe body panels, hinges, seals and glass, and the causes of corrosion.","Remove and refit interior trim without damage and fit accessories safely.","Recognise a collision-damaged or repaired vehicle and know when it is unsafe.","Trace the source of rattles, wind noise and water leaks in a logical way."],
  "cheatsheet": ["A seat belt that has been in a crash must be replaced, even if it looks fine.","Pretensioners and airbags are explosive devices. Treat them as live until they are fired or removed correctly.","Before airbag work, switch off the ignition, disconnect the battery and wait for the time in the manual.","Never probe SRS wiring with a test lamp or an ohmmeter. Use only the scan tool.","Carry a live airbag with the cover pointing away from your body and store it face up.","Use plastic trim tools and find every clip before you pull a panel off.","Water leaks follow the path of least resistance. The wet spot is rarely the entry point.","A vehicle with a bent frame, cracked welds or a repaired airbag may be unsafe even if it drives.","Do not fit extra wiring into the airbag or the main harness. Use a fused feed and a relay."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m20-l1", "title": "Seat belts and restraints", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"In a crash, the seat belt is the most effective safety device in the vehicle. It holds the occupant in the seat and spreads the force over the strong parts of the body. Airbags work with the belt, not instead of it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types and parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Three-point belt:** a belt that crosses the chest and the lap. It is the standard in all main seats.","**Inertia reel:** a reel that locks when the belt is pulled quickly or the car brakes hard or tilts.","**Pretensioner:** a device that pulls the belt tight in the first moments of a crash. Most use a small explosive charge fired by the airbag ECU.","**Load limiter:** lets the belt pay out a little at high force, so that the force on the chest is lower.","**Buckle and anchor points:** the buckle is sometimes fitted with a switch for the seat belt warning light."]},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"restraint-sequence"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Child restraints"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Children must use a restraint that suits their size and age, and not the adult belt alone. **ISOFIX** anchor points let a child seat clip rigidly into the seat frame. Follow the seat maker's instructions and the vehicle manual. Rear-facing seats must never be used in front of an active passenger airbag unless it is switched off."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inspection"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Pull the belt out fully and check the webbing for cuts, fraying, stiffness, burns or fading.","Check that the belt retracts smoothly and locks when pulled quickly.","Check the buckle clicks in and releases, and that the tongue is not worn.","Check the anchor bolts and mounting points for rust, cracks and looseness.","Check for the SRS or seat belt warning light on the dash."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"After a crash"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A belt that has been under a crash load is stretched inside, even if it looks normal, and it must be replaced. A belt with a fired pretensioner also needs replacement. Do not repair a belt by sewing or adjusting it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Belt warning and fault finding"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause","Action"],"rows":[["Belt warning light stays on","Buckle switch fault, seat occupancy sensor fault, or a fault in the restraint system","Read the restraint codes. Do not guess."],["Belt will not retract","Dirty or twisted webbing, weak spring, or a damaged retractor","Check the path of the webbing. Replace the belt if the retractor is faulty."],["Belt will not lock in a sudden stop","Failed locking mechanism","Replace the belt. Never repair a retractor."],["Buckle will not latch or release","Dirt, a spilled drink, or a damaged tongue","Clean with warm water if the maker allows. Replace if damaged."],["Webbing frayed, cut or stiff with dirt","Wear, or damage from sharp edges","Replace the belt."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Never modify a belt, add a clip that lets it hang slack, or fit a belt from a different vehicle. A belt works only if it is held in the correct position with the correct anchor torque."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A pretensioner contains an explosive charge. Do not drop it, heat it or open it, and do not test it with a battery or a meter. Remove and store it as the manual says."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A belt that has been in a crash is replaced. A belt that looks fine may have lost most of its strength."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l2", "title": "Crash energy, crumple zones and how restraints work together", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A modern vehicle is designed as a system. The body absorbs energy, the safety cell protects the occupants, and the restraints hold people in the right place so the body can protect them. A technician who understands this can see why a repair method matters, and why a small change can have a large effect in a crash."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The energy in a crash"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Kinetic energy = ½ × mass × speed²","caption":"Mass in kilograms, speed in metres per second, energy in joules"},
      {"t":"example","title":"A 1,300 kg car at 50 km/h","steps":["50 km/h is 13.9 m/s. Energy = 0.5 × 1,300 × 13.9² = 0.5 × 1,300 × 193 = 125,600 J, about 126 kJ.","At 100 km/h (27.8 m/s) the energy is 0.5 × 1,300 × 772.8 = 502,000 J, four times as much for twice the speed.","This energy must go somewhere. In a crash it goes into bending metal, which is why the front of the car crumples."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why a longer stopping distance saves lives"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The force on the occupants depends on how quickly they stop. If the car stops over a longer distance, the force is smaller."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Crumple zone effect","steps":["A car at 13.9 m/s stops in 0.6 m of crumple. Average deceleration = v² ÷ (2 × distance) = 193 ÷ 1.2 = 161 m/s², or about 16 g.","If the front were rigid and stopped in 0.1 m, the deceleration would be 193 ÷ 0.2 = 965 m/s², nearly 100 g. That is not survivable.","The crumple zone is therefore designed to collapse in a controlled way. The central cell is made strong, so it does not collapse."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How the restraints share the load"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Job","Timing"],"rows":[["Pretensioner","Removes belt slack, pulling the occupant back into the seat","First 10 to 15 ms"],["Airbag","Spreads the load over the chest and head, and stops them hitting the wheel or dash","Fully inflated in about 30 ms"],["Load limiter","Lets the belt pay out a controlled amount, so the chest force is not too high","From about 30 to 60 ms"],["Seat and head restraint","Hold the body and support the head and neck","Throughout, and especially in a rear impact"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"All of these must work together. An airbag without a belt can injure, because an unbelted occupant may be thrown into the bag as it inflates. A belt that is too loose lets the body move too far. This is why every occupant must wear a belt, and why a repair that changes the seat, belt or airbag is a safety matter."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why repair method matters"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Crumple zones depend on the shape, thickness and strength of the metal. Using the wrong steel or the wrong welding method changes how the part collapses.","High-strength steels must not be heated or straightened as if they were mild steel. Follow the manufacturer's repair method.","Sensors in the crush zone must be fitted in the correct position and direction, or the airbags may fire at the wrong time.","A repaired vehicle must have the same strength as the original, and the maker's repair information must be followed."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Restraints, crumple zones and the safety cell are one system. Weakening one part reduces the protection of all."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a customer asks why you will not repair something cheaply, explain this: the cheap way may work normally but fail in a crash."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l3", "title": "Airbag systems", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The airbag system is also called the **SRS** (supplemental restraint system). It supports the seat belt and reduces injury in a crash. It is a safety system for the driver but a danger for the mechanic who works on it without care."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"srs-layout"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Crash sensors:** in the front and sides of the vehicle, plus sensors in the ECU, which detect the sudden slowing in a crash.","**SRS ECU:** reads the sensors and decides if and which airbags to fire. It also stores crash data and fault codes.","**Inflator:** a small charge that makes gas in a fraction of a second.","**Airbags:** nylon bags in the steering wheel, the dashboard, the seats, the doors and the roof (curtains).","**Clock spring:** a flat coiled cable that carries the signal to the steering wheel airbag and the switches while the wheel turns.","**Warning light:** comes on at start, goes out after a few seconds, and stays on if there is a fault."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Precautions"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch off the ignition and **disconnect the battery**. Wait for the time stated in the manual so the stored energy can drain.","Do not use a test lamp or an ohmmeter on any SRS wiring or any airbag part. Use only the scan tool or the tool that the manual recommends.","Do not apply heat, hammer or open a live airbag or a pretensioner.","Carry a live airbag with the cover **away from your body** and store it face up on a flat surface, in a safe place away from heat.","Never fit a second-hand or unknown airbag, and do not fit a part from a vehicle in an accident.","Reconnect the battery with nobody in the vehicle and with the ignition off, then switch on and check the light."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fired or faulty airbags"},
      {"t":"p","text":"After a crash, all the fired airbags, the pretensioners, the sensors that were hit and usually the SRS ECU must be replaced as the manual says. A fired unit can leave a powder that can irritate skin, so wear gloves and wash your hands. A deployed or damaged unit must be disposed of through an approved route, not in a bin."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading the warning lamp"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the ignition is switched on, the airbag lamp lights for a few seconds while the module tests the system, then goes out. If it stays on, flashes, or does not light at all, the system may not fire in a crash. Treat every such fault as a safety defect. Tell the customer that the airbags may not work, and record it on the job card."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use a test lamp, multimeter or a probe on airbag wiring or connectors. Use only the diagnostic tool, and follow the manufacturer's workshop manual for how long to wait after disconnecting the battery."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"An airbag can deploy by accident if it is probed, heated or given the wrong signal, and the force can injure or kill. Follow the manual every time and, if you are not trained, do not touch it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"SRS faults are not repaired by guessing. Read the codes, follow the manual and replace parts with new ones."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l4", "title": "Airbag systems in depth: sensors, modules and service", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An airbag system is a small computer network with explosive parts. It decides in a few milliseconds whether a crash is serious enough to fire, and which bags to fire. This lesson explains how it decides and what a technician may and may not do."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sensors and the control module"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Position","Job"],"rows":[["Central crash sensor and control module","In the centre of the car, under the console","Measures deceleration in more than one direction, decides what to fire, stores crash data"],["Front impact sensors","Behind the front bumper or on the front crossmember","Detect an early frontal crash"],["Side impact sensors","In the doors or pillars, using pressure or acceleration","Detect side crashes quickly, as there is little crush space"],["Occupant sensors","In the seat","Detect whether a seat is occupied, and sometimes the weight, to turn the passenger bag on or off"],["Buckle switches","In the belt buckle","Tell the module if the belt is fastened"],["Rollover sensor","In the module","Triggers curtain bags and belt pretensioners in a roll"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What fires and when"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Crash","Likely response"],"rows":[["Light impact, low speed","No bag. Perhaps a belt pretensioner only."],["Moderate frontal impact","Pretensioners, and possibly a first stage of the front bags"],["Severe frontal impact","Pretensioners, both stages of the front bags, knee bags if fitted"],["Side impact","Side and curtain bags and pretensioners on the struck side"],["Rollover","Curtain bags and pretensioners"]],"caption":"Many modules use two stages for each front bag. The second stage is delayed or not used in a lower-speed crash or for a small or out-of-position occupant."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inside the inflator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the module fires a bag, it sends a small current through the **squib**, a tiny heating element in the inflator. This ignites a propellant. The gas fills the bag in about 30 milliseconds. The bag has vents that let the gas out as the occupant sinks into it, so it acts as a cushion. After the bag is deployed, the inflator and the bag are hot, and powder residue can irritate skin and eyes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Safe working on or near a restraint system"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch off the ignition, remove the key, and disconnect the battery. Wait the time that the manual says, because the module holds a charge.","Never carry a live airbag by its wires or with the cover facing you. Hold it close to your body with the cover facing away.","Store a live airbag cover up on a flat surface in a secure place, away from heat, and never stack anything on it.","Do not connect a battery, a charger or a test device to the airbag wiring. Use only the scan tool.","After the work, reconnect the battery with nobody in the car. Switch on, and check that the airbag light goes out.","Clear stored codes only after the fault has been repaired."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never drop, heat, drill, puncture or take apart an airbag, a pretensioner or an inflator. Never use an old or second-hand airbag from an unknown source. A deployed or damaged bag must be replaced and disposed of by the manufacturer's method."},
      {"t":"h","text":"After deployment"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Replace the airbags, the pretensioners and usually the belts that fired. Replace the control module if the maker says it stores crash data and cannot be reset.","Check the steering wheel, the clock spring, the seat frames and the sensors for damage. Replace damaged parts.","Inspect the wiring harness for burns and cuts.","Use a scan tool to check there are no codes, and that all parts report as present.","Tell the customer in writing what has been replaced."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The module decides, the sensors tell it, and the squib fires. Every part must be fitted correctly and have a clear signal."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A steering wheel airbag has a clock spring, which must be centred before it is fitted. A wheel fitted off-centre can break the clock spring and leave the airbag disabled."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l5", "title": "Body panels and glass", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The body protects the occupants and the parts, and a good body is a sign of a well-kept vehicle. A mechanic will often replace or adjust body parts as part of a repair."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Doors, bonnet and boot"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Hinges:** carry the weight and set the gap. Worn pins cause a door that drops and is hard to close.","**Latches and strikers:** hold the door shut. A misaligned striker gives wind noise and rattles.","**Gaps and alignment:** the gap between panels should be even. A change shows damage or wear.","**Gas struts or springs:** hold the bonnet or boot lid open. Never rely on them when working under a lid. Use the prop rod if there is one."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Seals and water leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Rubber **door seals** stop water and wind. They can harden, crack or shrink. Check them for tears and for gaps and clean the surfaces. Blocked drain holes at the bottom of doors and the sunroof frame cause water to fill and spill inside the car."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Glass"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The windscreen is bonded to the body and adds to the strength of the body, so a poorly fitted windscreen is a safety problem. The glass is toughened on the sides and laminated at the front. A small chip can be repaired, but a large crack or a crack in the driver's line of sight needs replacement. Use the right adhesive and allow it to cure before the vehicle is driven."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Corrosion"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Rust starts where paint is chipped, where water collects and where road salt or sea air is present. Stop it by cleaning, treating the metal and painting early. Check the sills, wheel arches, door bottoms and the floor. Structural rust in a sill or chassis is a safety problem."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When you remove a body part, bag and label the bolts and the clips, and photograph the gaps before you start. It makes the refit much easier."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A windscreen adhesive must cure for the time on the product before the car is driven, because the glass supports the roof and the airbag."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l6", "title": "Body construction, corrosion and sealing", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The body of a vehicle is made of many different metals, joined in different ways, with seals and coatings to keep water out. A technician working on trim, glass, doors or accessories needs to know how these parts fit, what damages them, and how to repair them correctly. This lesson covers construction, corrosion and sealing."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Body construction"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Description","Notes"],"rows":[["Monocoque (unibody)","The body shell is the structure. Floor, pillars, roof and bulkheads are welded together.","Almost all modern cars. Damage in one area spreads load to others."],["Body on frame","A separate steel ladder frame carries the engine, axles and body","Pickups, large SUVs and some vans. The frame can be checked and straightened."],["Space frame","A skeleton of tubes or extrusions with panels attached","Some aluminium and sports vehicles. Repair needs special methods."]]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Material","Where used","Notes"],"rows":[["Mild steel","Outer panels, floors","Easy to repair, but rusts"],["High-strength steel","Pillars, door beams, rails","Strong, thin, must not be heated or straightened without following the maker"],["Ultra-high-strength (boron) steel","Door intrusion beams, cabin cage","Cannot be repaired by heat. Replace in sections as the maker states."],["Aluminium","Bonnets, doors, whole bodies on some cars","Light. Needs separate tools, because iron dust causes corrosion."],["Plastics and composites","Bumpers, wings, trim","Many can be repaired with the right method; others must be replaced"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Corrosion in detail"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Steel rusts when oxygen and water reach the bare metal. Salt on the road, mud in the corners and trapped moisture speed it up. Rust grows from the inside out, so a small bubble on the paint can hide a large hole."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Where","What to do"],"rows":[["Surface rust","Paint chips, scratches","Clean, treat, prime and repaint"],["Scale or pitting","Wheel arches, door bottoms","Remove the rust fully, treat, prime and repaint"],["Perforation (holes)","Sills, floor, arch lips","Cut out and weld in new metal, or use a repair section. Do not fill with body filler alone."],["Crevice corrosion","Seams, behind trim","Open the seam, clean, and seal properly"],["Galvanic corrosion","Where aluminium meets steel","Keep the two metals separated by sealant or an insulating washer"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Rust on a structural part, such as a sill, a chassis rail, a suspension mounting or a seat belt anchor, makes the vehicle unsafe. Report it clearly and do not allow the vehicle to leave without telling the customer in writing."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sealing and water paths"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Seam sealer** is applied to joints in panels to keep water out. After welding, reapply it in the same pattern.","**Underbody coating and stone-chip protection** guard the floor and sills. Repair them after any work on the underside.","**Cavity wax** is sprayed inside sills and door skins. It displaces water, but the drain holes must be left open.","**Door and boot seals** (weatherstrips) must be clean, undamaged and seated. A seal that has flattened or split lets in water and wind noise.","**Grommets and bungs** in the floor close holes. Missing ones let in water and fumes."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Tracing a wet carpet","steps":["A customer finds the front passenger carpet wet after rain, but not after washing. Water sources include a windscreen seal, a sunroof drain, the scuttle drain, a door seal and the heater box.","Remove the carpet corner and look for a stain or water trail. Water often travels along a wire or a seam away from the leak.","Test with a hose, starting low and working upward, with a helper inside. Do not spray at the top first, or you will not know where it entered.","Often the scuttle drain, under the bonnet at the base of the windscreen, is blocked with leaves. Clearing it cures the problem."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Rust and leaks are caused by water. Find where it comes in and where it goes, and keep the drains open."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"When welding or cutting near fuel lines, airbags, a battery or foam, follow the manual. Disconnect the battery and shield the parts. Never weld or grind near a fuel tank that has not been made safe."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l7", "title": "Interior trim and accessories", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Removing trim is a daily job, and damaged clips and scratched panels make a customer unhappy. A little care and the right tools make a neat job."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Removing trim without damage"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Find the manual or a guide for the vehicle. Look for hidden screws behind covers and badges.","Use **plastic trim tools** and not a screwdriver, to avoid marks.","Release the clips by pulling close to each clip. Do not pull a panel from one corner.","Disconnect connectors for switches, speakers and lights before removing the panel completely.","Place parts in order and keep the clips and screws in a labelled tray."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fasteners"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many trim parts use plastic push clips, fir-tree clips and tabs. They break easily when cold or old, so keep a box of spares. Replace broken ones, since a missing clip causes rattles."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Mirrors, sunroofs and power windows"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Power mirrors and windows use small motors. If a window is slow or stops, check the switch, the fuse, the motor and the regulator, in that order. A sunroof needs clean and lightly greased tracks, and clear drain tubes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Accessories"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Tow bars and roof racks:** use the correct kit for the vehicle, and tighten to the specified torque. Do not exceed the maximum load.","**Audio and alarms:** the main risk is wiring. Use a fused feed taken from the battery or fuse box and a relay for high-current items. Do not cut into the main harness, and never connect to airbag wiring.","**Lights and accessories:** the total current must stay within the fuse and wire rating."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Poor aftermarket wiring causes many fires and battery drains. Use crimped or soldered and sealed joints, and place wires where they will not rub or get hot."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph the connectors and the routing before removing a panel. A phone picture saves a lot of time during assembly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Do not fit anything into the dashboard, door or seat areas where an airbag is. A badly placed accessory can turn into a projectile when the airbag fires."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l8", "title": "Electrical accessories, comfort systems and fitting extras", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Customers often ask for accessories: a tow bar, a tracker, extra lights, a dashcam, a stereo, parking sensors or roof bars. Each involves the body, the wiring and sometimes the safety systems. A technician who fits them correctly protects the vehicle and the customer. A poor installation causes electrical faults, fires, flat batteries and failed inspections."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Planning an installation"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Find out what the customer wants and what the vehicle already has. Some accessories need a particular option or software.","Check the manufacturer's information for approved parts and fitting points.","Decide where the wires will run, where the power comes from, and where the unit will be mounted.","Calculate the current and choose the correct fuse and wire size.","Fit, test, and write down what you did, so that the next technician can find it."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Choosing wire and fuse"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Load current","Minimum wire size (copper, short run)","Fuse"],"rows":[["Up to 5 A","0.5 mm²","7.5 A"],["Up to 10 A","1.0 mm²","15 A"],["Up to 15 A","1.5 mm²","20 A"],["Up to 25 A","2.5 mm²","30 A"],["Up to 40 A","4.0 mm²","50 A"]],"caption":"Values are a guide for short runs. Longer runs need thicker wire to limit voltage drop. Follow the accessory maker's instructions."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Fitting extra driving lights","steps":["Two 55 W lamps on a 12 V system draw 110 ÷ 12 = 9.2 A. A 15 A fuse is correct, and 1.5 mm² wire is a safe size for a short run.","Take the power through a **relay** switched by a small current from the dash switch, so the heavy current does not run through the switch.","Fuse the supply **close to the battery or the power source**, so that a short in the wire is protected along its whole length.","Run the wires away from sharp edges and heat, in a loom with grommets where they pass through metal.","Check that the lamps are aimed and are legal for road use in the area."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Where to take power from"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Source","Good for","Notes"],"rows":[["Switched fuse-box tap","Accessories that should turn off with the ignition","Use a correct fuse tap, with its own fuse"],["Permanent live","Trackers, dashcams with parking mode","Draws current when parked. Check battery drain."],["Battery directly via a fuse","High-current items","Fuse close to the battery"],["Never","Splicing into airbag wiring, CAN bus wires or sensor wires","Can disable a safety system or upset the network"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common accessories"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Accessory","Key points"],"rows":[["Tow bar","Fit to the maker's mounting points, torque the bolts, and wire the trailer socket using the correct module. Check the rating."],["Parking sensors","Mount at the correct height and angle, away from bumper joins. Calibrate if needed."],["Reversing camera","Wire to the reverse lamp circuit, and seal the entry point of the wire"],["Roof bars","Fit to the correct points and stay within the roof load limit"],["Tracker or alarm","Hide the unit, fuse the supply, and check the parasitic drain"],["Audio","Use proper fused power, a good earth, and avoid running signal cables beside power cables"],["Tinted film","Check the legal limit for light passing through"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking after installation"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Test every new function and every existing function that could have been affected, such as lights, windows, locks and the horn.","Look for new fault codes with a scan tool.","Measure the **parasitic drain** with the vehicle asleep. It should usually be under about 50 mA after the modules have shut down (check the manual).","Secure all cables and cover joints with tape or heat shrink.","Give the customer the instructions and a list of what was fitted."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Every accessory needs a fuse near the supply, a wire of the right size and a clean, secure route."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never fit accessories in a way that blocks an airbag or covers a sensor. Never tap into airbag, braking or steering circuits. Disconnect the battery before you start."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l9", "title": "Collision-damaged vehicles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The body of a modern car is built to crush in a crash in a planned way and to protect the passengers. Damage and repair therefore matter much more than the look of the panels."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Structure"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Crumple zones:** the front and rear are designed to collapse and absorb energy.","**Safety cell:** the passenger area is strong and stiff and is made to stay intact.","**High-strength steel:** parts of the frame use special steels that must be repaired in the way the maker states, and often cannot be heated or straightened."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Signs of past accident damage"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Uneven gaps between panels, or doors that do not close well.","Different paint colour or texture, overspray on rubber seals and trim.","Welds, fresh seam sealer or sealant that does not match the others.","Bolts with a wrench mark or paint cracks on the front wings and the suspension mountings.","Tyres that wear unevenly, or a car that pulls, which can show a bent frame.","Airbag light on, or an airbag cover that does not match."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"When the vehicle is unsafe"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A car with a bent frame or a damaged suspension mounting, a cracked weld, a repaired airbag with unknown parts, or a missing airbag is not safe. If you see these signs, tell the customer plainly, in writing, and do not hide it. Working with a good **body shop** that uses a frame measuring system gives the best result."},
      {"t":"h","text":"After a crash"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Replace the fired airbags, the pretensioners, the damaged belts and any sensors, as the manual says. Check the wheel alignment and the steering, and reset the SRS system and check that the warning light goes out."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The look of a car does not show how safe it is. A good-looking car with a bent structure can fail in the next crash."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Fuel and electrical systems can be damaged in a crash. Look for fuel leaks and damaged high-voltage cables on hybrid and electric cars before you start any work."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l10", "title": "Assessing damage, repairability and recording", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"When a vehicle comes in after a collision or with unknown history, the technician has to decide what is damaged, what is safe, and what must be repaired or replaced. This lesson shows a methodical way to assess damage and to record it so that the customer, insurer and the next technician can trust it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Step-by-step assessment"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Walk around the vehicle. Photograph all sides, and note any damage, paint differences, uneven panel gaps or fluid on the ground.","Check the **gaps** between panels. They should be even. A tapered gap shows that something has moved.","Check doors, bonnet and boot for correct opening and closing.","Look under the bonnet and under the car for bent rails, cracked mounts, damaged suspension and leaks.","Check the **wheels and tyres**. Bent wheels, uneven wear or a wheel pushed back show impact.","Read all stored fault codes. Look at the airbag and ABS systems.","If the structure is damaged, the vehicle should be measured on a body measuring system against the maker's data.","Road test, if it is safe to do so, to check steering, braking and tracking."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Signs of structural damage"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Sign","What it may mean"],"rows":[["Uneven panel gaps","A shifted panel or a bent body shell"],["Creased or wrinkled floor or rails","Energy has gone into the structure"],["Sealer or spot welds that look different","Panels have been replaced"],["Doors that drop or bind","Pillar or hinge damage"],["Vehicle pulls and tyres wear unevenly","Bent suspension or an out-of-line subframe"],["Overspray on rubbers, wiring or trim","Paintwork done after a repair"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Repair or replace?"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Usually"],"rows":[["Outer panel with a shallow dent","Repair"],["Panel with a sharp crease or stretched metal","Replace"],["Bumper cover with a small scratch","Repair and repaint"],["Bumper reinforcement, crash beam","Replace if deformed"],["Suspension arm, knuckle, subframe","Replace if bent. Never straighten."],["Wheel with a bend or crack","Replace"],["Seat belt that was worn in a crash","Replace"],["Structural rail","Replace the section, or repair per maker information"]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Estimating the repair time","steps":["A repair has: front bumper replace 2.0 h, bonnet replace 1.5 h, headlamp replace 0.8 h, paint 4.0 h, wheel alignment 1.0 h. The total labour is 9.3 h.","At a labour rate of 60 per hour the labour cost is 558. Add parts, paint materials and tax.","Hidden damage found after dismantling must be reported to the customer or insurer before extra work is done."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Recording the work"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Take photographs before, during and after.","Write the faults found, the measurements and the repair method, with part numbers.","Keep scan reports before and after.","Keep the customer's written approval for extra work."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A good assessment is organised, measured and recorded. Never guess at hidden damage."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A vehicle with a damaged structure, steering, brakes, fuel system or airbags must not be driven until it is made safe. A high-voltage vehicle that has been in a crash needs the high-voltage system checked by a trained person before anyone works on it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l11", "title": "Noise, vibration and water leaks", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Many customers come to the workshop with a rattle, a squeak or a wet carpet, and these are hard faults, because they are not always present. A patient and logical approach is needed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Finding noises"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Ask the customer **when** and **where**: cold or hot, over bumps or turning, which seat.","Reproduce the noise on a road test, with a helper if possible.","Narrow it down by pressing on parts, or by using a **mechanic's stethoscope** or a length of hose to the ear.","Take away loose items in the glove box and boot, since they can be the cause.","Fix the source with felt tape, a new clip, a bit of lubricant or a replacement part."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Noise","Common causes"],"rows":[["Rattle","Loose trim, a heat shield, a loose exhaust hanger, a worn striker"],["Squeak","Dry bushes, rubbing plastic trim, door seals, a dry hinge"],["Whistle or wind noise","A worn seal, a misaligned door or window, a roof rack, a gap in a body panel"],["Hum or drone","Tyres, wheel bearings, an exhaust resonance"]],"caption":"Noise causes"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Water leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Water follows gravity and the paths of least resistance. The wet spot on the carpet is rarely the place where the water came in. Check in this order: door seals, windscreen and rear glass seals, sunroof drains, the scuttle (the area below the windscreen), the boot seal, and the tail lamps. Test by pouring water in a **gentle stream from a hose, starting low and working upward**, with a person inside. Do not use a high pressure jet at the seals."},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **drain holes** at the bottom of the doors and the sunroof tubes can be cleaned with a thin wire or compressed air. A blocked drain is a very common cause. Air-conditioning drain water under the car is normal, but water inside the carpet by the front passenger foot can come from a blocked AC drain or a leaking heater matrix, which smells sweet."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Fix leaks early. Water causes rust, mould, electrical faults and damage to the ECUs that are often under the carpets."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A damp smell and a window that fogs easily often point to a water leak or a blocked drain."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m20-l12", "title": "Noise, vibration and harshness: a systematic approach", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Noises and vibrations are among the most difficult complaints because they come and go, and customers describe them in different ways. A methodical approach saves hours. This lesson gives a way to describe, reproduce, locate and fix a noise or a vibration."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Describe the complaint"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Question","Why it helps"],"rows":[["What does it sound like: rattle, squeak, hum, thump, whistle or click?","Different types point to different causes"],["When does it happen: cold, hot, wet, dry?","Rubber and plastic change with temperature"],["At what speed or engine rpm?","Speed-related points to wheels or driveline. Rpm-related points to the engine."],["Does it change when braking, turning or going over bumps?","Brakes, steering or suspension"],["Where is it from, front, rear, left or right?","Narrows the search"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of noise and likely sources"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Noise","Common sources"],"rows":[["Rattle","Loose trim, a heat shield, an exhaust hanger, a loose item in the boot"],["Squeak","Rubber bushes, door seals, brake pads, belts"],["Hum or drone","Tyres, wheel bearings, exhaust resonance"],["Clunk over bumps","Drop links, strut top mounts, bushes, steering joints"],["Whistle","Wind around a door seal or mirror, a vacuum leak, a worn belt tensioner"],["Click on turning","A worn CV joint"],["Wind noise","A door seal that is out of place, or a poorly fitted glass or mirror"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Vibration and its frequency"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A vibration has a **frequency**, the number of times it repeats in a second. Matching the frequency to a rotating part finds the source."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Frequency (Hz) = revolutions per minute ÷ 60","caption":"A part spinning at 600 rpm vibrates 10 times a second"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Which part causes the shake?","steps":["A car has a shake at 100 km/h. The tyre has a rolling circumference of 2.0 m. Wheel speed = 27.8 ÷ 2.0 = 13.9 revolutions per second, or 13.9 Hz.","If the vibration frequency is 13.9 Hz, it is once per wheel revolution, so look for a **wheel or tyre out of balance**.","If the frequency was twice this, the cause could be a second-order effect such as a tyre out of round.","The propshaft turns faster than the wheels, because of the final drive ratio. A propshaft fault gives a higher frequency, at a ratio of the final drive.","An engine vibration changes with engine rpm, not with road speed, and it is also present when the vehicle is stationary."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Finding the cause"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Reproduce the noise on a road test with the customer if possible.","Change one thing at a time. For example, apply the brake lightly, shift into neutral and coast, or press the clutch.","Use a **chassis ear**, a stethoscope or listening tubes to find the source.","Press or wiggle parts by hand. Lubricate the suspected rubber with silicone spray, and see if the squeak stops.","For rattles in the cabin, wedge foam or cloth between panels to quiet them one at a time.","Confirm the repair on the same road test."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Describe, reproduce, locate, fix and re-test. Change one variable at a time."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a vibration changes when you shift into neutral at the same speed, it is from the engine or the transmission. If it does not, it is from the wheels, tyres or driveshafts."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Door trim removal and an SRS awareness inspection","objective":"Remove and refit a door trim panel with the correct clips and test a power window, and carry out an SRS awareness inspection that stops before touching any airbag part.","time":"75 minutes","tools":["Training vehicle with a power window door","Plastic trim removal tools, Phillips and Torx screwdrivers","Trays for screws and clips, and a supply of spare clips","Multimeter for the window circuit only","Workshop manual and a pen"],"safety":["Do not touch, test or remove any airbag, pretensioner or SRS wiring in this exercise.","Switch off the ignition. Disconnect the battery and wait the time in the manual before removing a door panel that holds a side airbag.","Wear gloves, as door edges and glass can be sharp.","Do not pull the panel by force. Find the hidden screws and clips first."],"steps":["Read the manual for the door trim removal procedure.","Note the position of the airbag, the SRS warning light and the airbag labels. Record the SRS light state on starting.","Switch off and disconnect the battery. Wait the time that the manual gives.","Remove the covers and the screws from the door handle and the armrest.","Release the clips around the edge of the panel with the plastic trim tool.","Disconnect the connectors from the switches, speakers and lights.","Inspect the clips, the vapour barrier and the drain holes, and clear any blocked holes.","Reconnect the battery, with the connectors fitted, and test the window with the switch. If it is slow, check the fuse, the voltage at the motor and the regulator.","Refit the panel with new clips if any are broken, and check that the gap is even.","Re-check the window, the lock and the SRS light.","Write a short note of what you found and did."],"checks":["I kept away from the airbag and SRS wiring.","I removed the panel with the right tools and without damage.","I labelled and stored the screws and clips.","I tested the window and found the cause of any fault in order.","I checked that the SRS light went out."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m20-q01","q":"What is a pretensioner?","options":["A device that releases the seat belt slowly during a crash","A device that pulls the seat belt tight in the first moments of a crash","A spring that retracts the belt when it is not in use by the driver","A switch that turns on the seat belt warning light on the dash"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l1","explain":"Most use a small explosive charge fired by the airbag ECU, so handle them with care."},
    {"id":"m20-q02","q":"What does a load limiter do?","options":["It lets the belt pay out a little at high force to reduce the load on the chest","It measures the weight of the person who sits in the seat","It stops the belt from locking when the car brakes hard on a road","It stops the buckle from releasing after a crash has happened"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l1","explain":"It reduces the injury caused by the belt itself."},
    {"id":"m20-q03","q":"What is ISOFIX?","options":["A buckle that locks the belt around a baby in a front seat","A set of anchor points for fitting a child seat rigidly to the vehicle","A type of curtain airbag fitted in the roof above the rear seats of a car","A test for the strength of the seat belt webbing in the workshop"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l1","explain":"Follow the seat maker's and the vehicle manual's instructions."},
    {"id":"m20-q04","q":"A belt has been in a crash and looks fine. What should be done?","options":["Keep it, because it looks undamaged on the outside of the webbing","Sew the frayed parts and refit it to the vehicle seat","Wash it and let it dry in the sun for a full day","Replace it, because it may have lost its strength inside"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l1","explain":"Webbing that has been stretched cannot be trusted."},
    {"id":"m20-q05","q":"What may a rear-facing child seat never be used in front of?","options":["A seat with a head restraint fitted","A seat with a three-point belt","An active passenger airbag","A seat with an ISOFIX anchor point"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l1","explain":"An opening airbag can hit the seat and seriously injure the child."},
    {"id":"m20-q06","q":"What happens to kinetic energy when the speed doubles?","options":["It doubles as well","It stays the same","It halves","Four times as great"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l2","explain":"Kinetic energy is half mass times speed squared, so doubling the speed gives four times the energy."},
    {"id":"m20-q07","q":"Why is a crumple zone useful?","options":["Longer stopping distance, lower force","It makes the car lighter","It keeps the engine cool for longer","It stops all damage to the body"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l2","explain":"A longer stopping distance means a lower deceleration for the occupants, so less force on the body."},
    {"id":"m20-q08","q":"Which restraint part removes belt slack first?","options":["The airbag","The pretensioner","The load limiter","The head restraint"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l2","explain":"The pretensioner fires in the first few milliseconds and pulls the occupant back into the seat."},
    {"id":"m20-q09","q":"In a crash, what is the job of the load limiter in a belt?","options":["Locks the belt completely","Warns the driver of danger","Releases a little webbing","Fires the airbag module"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l2","explain":"By releasing a little webbing, it limits the force on the chest so that the belt itself does not injure."},
    {"id":"m20-q10","q":"Why must high-strength steel repairs follow the maker's method?","options":["Heat can weaken the steel","The paint will not stick well","It is lighter than aluminium","It cannot be measured at all"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l2","explain":"High-strength steels lose strength when overheated or straightened wrongly, which changes how the body behaves in a crash."},
    {"id":"m20-q11","q":"What does SRS stand for?","options":["Safe running system","Supplemental restraint system","Standard roll sensor","Seat rotation switch"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l3","explain":"It is the airbag system that works with the seat belts."},
    {"id":"m20-q12","q":"Which part carries the signal to the steering wheel airbag while the wheel turns?","options":["The inflator in the dashboard","The clock spring","The crash sensor in the bumper","The ignition lock cylinder"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l3","explain":"It is a flat coiled cable. Do not turn it when the wheel is removed."},
    {"id":"m20-q13","q":"What is the first step before working near the airbags?","options":["Switch off the ignition, disconnect the battery and wait the time in the manual","Test the airbag wires with a test lamp or a meter to check that they are dead","Turn the ignition on and check that the SRS light comes on and goes out","Start the engine so that the airbag system is fully charged"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l3","explain":"The stored energy needs time to drain away."},
    {"id":"m20-q14","q":"Why should SRS wiring never be probed with an ohmmeter or a test lamp?","options":["The system will lose its saved codes","The current may fire the airbag by mistake","The wires are too thin for the probes","The meter will be damaged by the airbag"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l3","explain":"Use the scan tool or the tool that the manual states."},
    {"id":"m20-q15","q":"How should a live airbag be carried?","options":["With the cover pointing down to the floor","With the cover held against your chest","With the cover pointing away from your body","Wrapped in a cloth and thrown into a box"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l3","explain":"If it fired, the cover would move away from you. Store it face up in a safe place."},
    {"id":"m20-q16","q":"Which parts should usually be replaced after an airbag has fired?","options":["Only the airbag cover and the trim, since the rest of the system is safe to reuse","The airbags, the pretensioners, the damaged sensors and usually the SRS ECU","Only the steering wheel and the dashboard top where the airbag sat","Nothing else, if the SRS warning light has gone out after the crash"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l3","explain":"Follow the manual, since the ECU stores crash data."},
    {"id":"m20-q17","q":"What lights the propellant in an airbag inflator?","options":["A spark plug","A squib","A glow plug","A fuse link"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l4","explain":"The control module sends a small current to the squib, which heats and ignites the propellant."},
    {"id":"m20-q18","q":"How fast does a front airbag inflate?","options":["In about 3 seconds","In about 30 seconds","In about 30 milliseconds","In about 3 minutes"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l4","explain":"The airbag must be full before the occupant moves forward, so it inflates in around 30 ms."},
    {"id":"m20-q19","q":"Which way does the cover face when you carry a live airbag module?","options":["Towards your chest","Down, hanging by wires","Towards your face","Away from your body"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l4","explain":"If it fired, the cover would move away from you. Never carry it by the wires."},
    {"id":"m20-q20","q":"What should be done before working on an airbag system?","options":["Test the wires with a test lamp","Disconnect the battery and wait","Leave the ignition switched on","Tap the module to test it"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l4","explain":"The module stores energy after power is removed. The manual gives the safe waiting time."},
    {"id":"m20-q21","q":"The airbag lamp stays on after start-up. What does this mean?","options":["It is working perfectly","The bulb is simply old","The system may not fire","The seat is too far back"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l4","explain":"A lamp that stays lit shows the module found a fault. It is a safety defect that needs a scan tool check."},
    {"id":"m20-q22","q":"Why must a windscreen be allowed to cure before driving?","options":["Because the wiper arms cannot be moved while the adhesive is wet","It is bonded to the body and supports the roof and the airbag","Because the adhesive creates a bad smell inside for a day or two","Because the glass will be too heavy for the frame until it has dried"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l5","explain":"Follow the cure time on the adhesive before the vehicle is driven."},
    {"id":"m20-q23","q":"What may cause a door to drop and be hard to close?","options":["A flat battery in the car","Dirty windscreen wipers on the front glass","A blocked fuel filter in the engine","Worn hinge pins or bushes"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l5","explain":"Hinges carry the weight and set the gap."},
    {"id":"m20-q24","q":"Where is rust most likely to start?","options":["On the plastic bumper covers","On the inside of the engine block","Where paint is chipped, or water and salt collect","On the clean top of the roof only"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l5","explain":"Check the sills, wheel arches and door bottoms, and treat the rust early."},
    {"id":"m20-q25","q":"What do blocked door drain holes cause?","options":["The windows go up and down more slowly than they did before","Water collects in the door and may spill inside or cause rust","The radio loses its signal when the doors are shut firmly","The door locks freeze in cold weather and cannot be opened"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l5","explain":"Clean them with a thin wire or compressed air so the water can drain."},
    {"id":"m20-q26","q":"Which body type uses the shell as the structure?","options":["Ladder frame","Space frame","Chassis cab","Monocoque"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l6","explain":"A monocoque or unibody uses the welded shell for the strength, instead of a separate frame."},
    {"id":"m20-q27","q":"Why must iron dust be kept away from aluminium?","options":["It causes corrosion","It makes it softer","It improves welding","It changes the colour"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l6","explain":"Steel dust on aluminium sets up galvanic corrosion, so aluminium work needs separate tools."},
    {"id":"m20-q28","q":"What should be done about rust that has made a hole in a sill?","options":["Fill with body filler only","Paint over it and leave","Ignore it as cosmetic","Weld in new metal"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l6","explain":"Filler does not restore strength. A sill is structural, and needs a proper weld repair."},
    {"id":"m20-q29","q":"A customer has a wet carpet after rain. What should be checked first?","options":["The scuttle drain","The engine oil level","The tyre pressures","The battery terminals"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l6","explain":"A blocked drain, a split seal or a sunroof drain are the most common causes. Test with a hose, working from low to high."},
    {"id":"m20-q30","q":"What tool should be used to release plastic trim panels?","options":["A plastic trim removal tool","A hammer and chisel at the corner of the panel","A flat steel screwdriver pushed behind the panel","Pliers to pull the panel by its edge"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l7","explain":"It avoids marks and breakage of the clips."},
    {"id":"m20-q31","q":"A power window is slow. Which is the best order of checks?","options":["Change the battery and the alternator before any other check","Replace the motor first and then test the switch, the fuse and the wiring","The switch and fuse, the voltage at the motor, then the motor and regulator","Replace the regulator first and then check the fuse and the motor"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l7","explain":"Check the simple and cheap parts first, then work towards the dearer ones."},
    {"id":"m20-q32","q":"How should extra accessory wiring be powered?","options":["From a fused supply, with a relay for high-current items","By joining it to the airbag wiring since it is close","By joining it to the nearest wire with a twisted joint","By cutting into the main harness and joining wires with tape"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l7","explain":"Poor wiring causes fires and a flat battery."},
    {"id":"m20-q33","q":"Why should nothing be fitted where an airbag is?","options":["It would make the dashboard look untidy and hard to clean afterwards","It would make the airbag cover change colour","It would slow the radio down","It can become a projectile when the airbag fires"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l7","explain":"Keep clear of the airbag areas, including the doors, seats and dashboard."},
    {"id":"m20-q34","q":"What is a roof rack's most important limit?","options":["The maximum load, in the manual for the vehicle and the rack","The weight of the rack bars on their own without a load","The number of bars that are fitted across the roof","The colour of the rack against the paint on the roof of the car"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l7","explain":"Overloading a roof raises the centre of gravity and affects handling."},
    {"id":"m20-q35","q":"Two 55 W lamps on 12 V draw how much current?","options":["About 1 A","About 90 A","About 9 A","About 0.9 A"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l8","explain":"Current = power ÷ voltage = 110 ÷ 12 = 9.2 A. A 15 A fuse and 1.5 mm² wire are suitable."},
    {"id":"m20-q36","q":"Why use a relay for high-power lamps?","options":["To make the lamps brighter","Keeps heavy current off the switch","To make the wire cheaper","To remove the need for a fuse"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l8","explain":"A small current switches the relay, which carries the big current. This protects the switch and the dash wiring."},
    {"id":"m20-q37","q":"Where should the fuse for a new accessory be placed?","options":["Near the power source","Close to the accessory","Anywhere along the wire","No fuse is needed"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l8","explain":"Fusing near the source protects the whole length of the wire if it shorts."},
    {"id":"m20-q38","q":"Should you splice into airbag wiring for a new accessory?","options":["Yes, if it is yellow","Never","Yes, with tape","Only on Sundays"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l8","explain":"Airbag circuits are safety critical. Any splice could disable the system or set it off."},
    {"id":"m20-q39","q":"What is a crumple zone?","options":["A part of the bumper that is made of soft rubber to protect the paint","A section of the exhaust that bends when the car is hit","A part of the body designed to collapse and absorb the crash energy","A zone of the glass that breaks into small pieces in a crash"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l9","explain":"The safety cell around the passengers is made to stay intact."},
    {"id":"m20-q40","q":"Which is a sign that a car was repaired after an accident?","options":["Uneven panel gaps, overspray on the seals and mismatched paint","A clean engine bay, shiny wheels and a freshly washed interior","A full tank of fuel, a clean interior and a service history","A new set of tyres of a good make on all four wheels"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l9","explain":"Look for welds, wrench marks on bolts and fresh sealer."},
    {"id":"m20-q41","q":"Why must high-strength steel be repaired as the maker says?","options":["It is too soft to be welded to the other body panels, so it must always be bonded","It cannot be painted after any repair has been completed","Heat or the wrong method can weaken it and it may not protect in the next crash","It always has to be replaced with a plastic part instead"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l9","explain":"Often these parts cannot be heated or straightened."},
    {"id":"m20-q42","q":"A car has a bent frame but drives well. What should the mechanic say?","options":["That it is fine because the car drives straight on the road","That only the paint needs to be repaired for safety in a crash","That the structure is damaged and the car may be unsafe in a crash","That a wheel alignment will cure the bent frame completely"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l9","explain":"Tell the customer plainly and record it in writing on the job card."},
    {"id":"m20-q43","q":"What is the most important check on a hybrid after a crash?","options":["The tyre tread depth and the tyre pressures on the wheels","The radio and the speakers in the doors and the dashboard","Damaged high-voltage cables or components, before any work is done","The colour of the paint on the repaired body panels of the car and the trim"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l9","explain":"Only trained persons work on high-voltage systems."},
    {"id":"m20-q44","q":"What does an uneven panel gap suggest?","options":["A perfect repair","A new paint colour","A shifted panel","A low tyre pressure"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l10","explain":"Even gaps show the shell is in line. A tapering gap shows something has moved."},
    {"id":"m20-q45","q":"Should a bent suspension arm be straightened?","options":["Yes, with a hammer","Yes, using heat","Only the left one","No, replace it"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l10","explain":"Straightening can weaken the metal. Safety parts are replaced, not straightened."},
    {"id":"m20-q46","q":"What must be done if hidden damage is found during a repair?","options":["Hide it from the owner","Report it first","Ignore it completely","Fit a cheaper part instead"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l10","explain":"The customer or insurer must approve extra work. Record it with photographs."},
    {"id":"m20-q47","q":"What is the first thing to ask about a rattle?","options":["When and where it happens, and under which conditions","How many people are in the vehicle every day","What colour the car is, and the age of the car","Where the customer bought the car originally"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l11","explain":"Hot or cold, over bumps or turning, and which part of the car."},
    {"id":"m20-q48","q":"Why is the wet carpet rarely where the leak enters?","options":["Because water always drips straight down to the lowest point at the entry","Because carpets pull water from the roof by themselves","Because leaks only occur at the floor of a car","Water follows the path of least resistance and travels"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l11","explain":"Trace the water upward to find the entry point."},
    {"id":"m20-q49","q":"How should you test for a water leak with a hose?","options":["Use a high-pressure jet straight at the seals from close, to find the gap","Start at the roof and then work down to the seals, with no helper","Pour water into the engine bay and wait overnight to see what happens","Use a gentle stream, starting low and working up, with a person inside"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l11","explain":"A strong jet can force water through good seals and mislead you."},
    {"id":"m20-q50","q":"What is a common cause of water leaks into the front passenger footwell?","options":["A blocked air-conditioning drain or a leaking heater matrix","A loose fuel cap or a dirty fuel filter in the fuel line","A weak horn or a broken indicator stalk in the column","A worn spark plug or a failing ignition coil on one cylinder"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l11","explain":"Sweet-smelling water with a fogging windscreen points to a heater matrix."},
    {"id":"m20-q51","q":"A customer complains of a rattle from the rear on rough roads. What is a sensible first check?","options":["Recalibrate the steering angle sensor with the scan tool","Replace the engine mounts on the front of the car at once","Change the engine oil and the filter before any other check","Loose items in the boot, the heat shield and the exhaust hangers"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l11","explain":"Easy and cheap checks come first, before any expensive parts are replaced."},
    {"id":"m20-q52","q":"Why should leaks be repaired early?","options":["Because water changes the sound of the horn","Because wet carpets make the car lighter","Because leaks make the paint a different colour","Water causes rust, mould and electrical faults"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l11","explain":"Many ECUs are under the carpets and are damaged by water."},
    {"id":"m20-q53","q":"A vibration at 100 km/h goes away when the car is in neutral at the same speed. Where is it from?","options":["The engine","The gearbox oil","Wheels, tyres or driveline","The exhaust tailpipe"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l12","explain":"If the engine is not driving and the vibration remains, it comes from the road-speed parts."},
    {"id":"m20-q54","q":"A wheel turns at 13.9 revolutions per second. What is the frequency?","options":["139 Hz","1.39 Hz","834 Hz","13.9 Hz"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m20-l12","explain":"One cycle per revolution, so the frequency in hertz equals the revolutions per second."},
    {"id":"m20-q55","q":"A click when turning on full lock is likely from what?","options":["A worn CV joint","A flat battery","A loose fuel cap","A leaking wiper"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m20-l12","explain":"A clicking noise on turning, worse on full lock, points to a worn outer CV joint."},
    {"id":"m20-q56","q":"What is the best approach to finding an unknown noise?","options":["Replace parts at random","Turn up the radio loudly","Change one thing at a time","Wait and see for a month"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m20-l12","explain":"Changing one variable at a time shows what affects the noise, so you narrow down the cause."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m20-c01","front":"What is a pretensioner?","back":"A device that pulls the belt tight at the start of a crash.","lesson":"m20-l1"},
    {"id":"m20-c02","front":"What is a load limiter?","back":"It lets the belt pay out a little at high force.","lesson":"m20-l1"},
    {"id":"m20-c03","front":"What is ISOFIX?","back":"Anchor points to fit a child seat rigidly.","lesson":"m20-l1"},
    {"id":"m20-c04","front":"What to do with a belt that has been in a crash?","back":"Replace it.","lesson":"m20-l1"},
    {"id":"m20-c05","front":"Kinetic energy formula?","back":"Half mass times speed squared. Double the speed, four times the energy.","lesson":"m20-l2"},
    {"id":"m20-c06","front":"Why do cars have crumple zones?","back":"They lengthen the stopping distance so that the force on the occupants is lower.","lesson":"m20-l2"},
    {"id":"m20-c07","front":"Pretensioner timing?","back":"Fires in the first 10 to 15 ms to remove belt slack.","lesson":"m20-l2"},
    {"id":"m20-c08","front":"What does SRS mean?","back":"Supplemental restraint system, the airbag system.","lesson":"m20-l3"},
    {"id":"m20-c09","front":"What carries the signal to the steering wheel airbag?","back":"The clock spring.","lesson":"m20-l3"},
    {"id":"m20-c10","front":"What is the first airbag precaution?","back":"Ignition off, disconnect the battery and wait.","lesson":"m20-l3"},
    {"id":"m20-c11","front":"Can you probe SRS wiring?","back":"No. Use only the scan tool.","lesson":"m20-l3"},
    {"id":"m20-c12","front":"How do you carry a live airbag?","back":"Cover pointing away from your body.","lesson":"m20-l3"},
    {"id":"m20-c13","front":"How do you store a live airbag?","back":"Face up on a flat surface, in a safe place away from heat.","lesson":"m20-l3"},
    {"id":"m20-c14","front":"What fires an inflator?","back":"A squib, heated by a current from the control module.","lesson":"m20-l4"},
    {"id":"m20-c15","front":"How fast is an airbag full?","back":"About 30 milliseconds.","lesson":"m20-l4"},
    {"id":"m20-c16","front":"After an airbag fires, what is replaced?","back":"Airbags, pretensioners, belts that fired, and often the module.","lesson":"m20-l4"},
    {"id":"m20-c17","front":"What do door hinges do?","back":"They carry the weight and set the gap.","lesson":"m20-l5"},
    {"id":"m20-c18","front":"What do blocked door drains cause?","back":"Water collects, rust and leaks.","lesson":"m20-l5"},
    {"id":"m20-c19","front":"Why let a windscreen adhesive cure?","back":"The glass supports the roof and the airbag.","lesson":"m20-l5"},
    {"id":"m20-c20","front":"Where does rust start?","back":"Where paint is chipped or water collects.","lesson":"m20-l5"},
    {"id":"m20-c21","front":"What is a monocoque?","back":"A body where the welded shell is the structure.","lesson":"m20-l6"},
    {"id":"m20-c22","front":"Why keep iron dust off aluminium?","back":"It causes galvanic corrosion.","lesson":"m20-l6"},
    {"id":"m20-c23","front":"Where is the scuttle drain?","back":"At the base of the windscreen, under the bonnet. It often blocks with leaves.","lesson":"m20-l6"},
    {"id":"m20-c24","front":"Which tool removes trim panels?","back":"A plastic trim tool.","lesson":"m20-l7"},
    {"id":"m20-c25","front":"How are trim clips released?","back":"By pulling close to each clip.","lesson":"m20-l7"},
    {"id":"m20-c26","front":"How do you power an accessory?","back":"From a fused supply with a relay for high current.","lesson":"m20-l7"},
    {"id":"m20-c27","front":"Why not fit accessories at an airbag?","back":"They can become projectiles.","lesson":"m20-l7"},
    {"id":"m20-c28","front":"How to calculate lamp current?","back":"Power divided by voltage. 110 W at 12 V is about 9 A.","lesson":"m20-l8"},
    {"id":"m20-c29","front":"Where is the fuse for an accessory placed?","back":"Close to the power source.","lesson":"m20-l8"},
    {"id":"m20-c30","front":"What is parasitic drain?","back":"Current drawn with the vehicle parked. Usually under about 50 mA once asleep.","lesson":"m20-l8"},
    {"id":"m20-c31","front":"What is a crumple zone?","back":"A part of the body made to collapse and absorb energy.","lesson":"m20-l9"},
    {"id":"m20-c32","front":"What is the safety cell?","back":"The strong passenger area made to stay intact.","lesson":"m20-l9"},
    {"id":"m20-c33","front":"Signs of accident repair?","back":"Uneven gaps, overspray, mismatched paint, fresh welds.","lesson":"m20-l9"},
    {"id":"m20-c34","front":"When is a car unsafe after a crash?","back":"With a bent structure, bad welds or unknown airbag parts.","lesson":"m20-l9"},
    {"id":"m20-c35","front":"What do uneven panel gaps show?","back":"A shifted panel or bent shell.","lesson":"m20-l10"},
    {"id":"m20-c36","front":"Should safety parts be straightened?","back":"No. Replace bent suspension arms and subframes.","lesson":"m20-l10"},
    {"id":"m20-c37","front":"What is the first question about a rattle?","back":"When and where it happens.","lesson":"m20-l11"},
    {"id":"m20-c38","front":"Where is a wet spot in relation to the leak?","back":"Rarely at the entry point.","lesson":"m20-l11"},
    {"id":"m20-c39","front":"How do you test for a leak with a hose?","back":"Gently, from low to high, with a person inside.","lesson":"m20-l11"},
    {"id":"m20-c40","front":"What cleans a drain hole?","back":"A thin wire or air.","lesson":"m20-l11"},
    {"id":"m20-c41","front":"What is a sweet smell and fogging?","back":"A leaking heater matrix.","lesson":"m20-l11"},
    {"id":"m20-c42","front":"Why repair leaks early?","back":"They cause rust, mould and electrical faults.","lesson":"m20-l11"},
    {"id":"m20-c43","front":"Frequency from rpm?","back":"Hertz equals revolutions per minute divided by 60.","lesson":"m20-l12"},
    {"id":"m20-c44","front":"Vibration stays in neutral. Source?","back":"Wheels, tyres or driveline, not the engine.","lesson":"m20-l12"},
    {"id":"m20-c45","front":"Where is a clicking on full lock from?","back":"A worn CV joint.","lesson":"m20-l12"}
  ]
});
